microtris - a complete Tetris in 209 bytes

microtris is Tetris as a 209-byte DOS .COM file. The smallest complete one I know of, pellsson's tinytris
(https://github.com/pellsson/tinytris), is 247. Both have the same six features: seven pieces, gravity, move, rotate,
line clearing, and a game over (a freeze). CLAIM says what "complete" means and why the line is there. There is no
record body for this: the claim is "209 bytes, six features, `make test` says so".

    $ make
    build/microtris.com: 209 bytes

make             build (needs nasm)
make play        play it in DOSBox (needs dosbox)
make quick       the checks on microtris only, a few minutes
make test        quick, then tinytris through the same checks (fetched, sha256 checked)
make matrix      the checks again with other registers at entry, load segments and seeds
make dosbox      the game under real DOSBox on a private X display
make holder-bug  the two defects found in tinytris
make speed       instructions per row of fall, both programs

The checks need python3 with unicorn and capstone (arch: python-unicorn python-capstone). make dosbox also needs
dosbox, xdotool, Xvfb, ImageMagick and Pillow.

keys

j left, l right, k rotate. Nothing else does anything. The fall speed is the CPU's: neither program has a timer.
make play fixes DOSBox at 400 cycles, about a row a second; Ctrl+F11 and Ctrl+F12 slow it down and speed it up. When
the stack reaches the top the program freezes with the board on screen; close the window.

how it got smaller

The design is pellsson's: one 16-bit word per row for the board, a piece as a 4x4 bit mask, one routine that tests,
stamps and erases by patching its own opcode, the result carried in the zero flag, undo through the stack. What
changed:

- Rotation by multiplication. A column of the mask is gathered into a nibble with one `and` and one `imul 0x1248`
  (the partial products never meet), four times round: 27 bytes instead of 46.
- One byte per piece. Every tetromino fits in two rows of its box, so the table is 7 bytes read with `xlat`.
- The colour comes from the flags. After `test [si],dx`, `lahf` already holds an attribute byte whose bit 6 is "was
  empty": red well, black blocks and walls, no compare, no jump.
- The board starts at offset 0 (the first 50 bytes of the PSP), so the row-shift loop ends when si reaches 0 and a
  negative si can mean game over.
- Smaller things: xor for both stamp and erase, jmp where tinytris pushes a return address, the shared routine clears
  its own accumulator, the board is walked with lodsw.

what the tests found in tinytris

It passes all six feature checks. Two other checks found defects (details in test/NOTES); reported to its author at
https://github.com/pellsson/tinytris/issues/1.

1. A vertical I vanishes at the right wall. `l` once too often takes the column shift to 0xff, the CPU masks the
   count, every cell shifts out, and a cell that is not there can never collide. test/fuzz.py (random keys, every
   frame judged) shows it. microtris moves the well so that shift 0 is never legal, and test/reach.py checks by
   exhaustive search that no reachable position loses a cell.
2. Its draw loop never ends where it says: `dec cx / jae` ignores the carry, so the loop stops only when the screen
   pointer wraps, about 410 rows later, after writing roughly 64 KB over the B800 segment every frame. The VM maps
   all memory as RAM, so the six checks cannot see it; test/footprint.py can. microtris had the same loop until that
   check caught it. It draws 25 rows now. A row of fall takes 315,265 instructions against tinytris's 355,481
   (make speed).

what this does not claim

- Not a record by anyone's authority: a number, a rule set and a harness you can run.
- Not tested on hardware: a deterministic VM on Unicorn (test/dosvm.py: no wall clock, scripted keys and timer) and
  one emulator, DOSBox, as a cross-check.
- 80186 instructions (pusha, imul with an immediate, shifts by immediate), like tinytris. `aam 7` is not base 7 on a
  NEC V20/V30, also like tinytris.
- It never returns to DOS, because its board sits on the PSP, and it does not clear the screen first.
- Prior art I know: tinytris (247), 4is256 (256, with scoring and colours), the Hugi Compo #22 winner (363), TetrOS
  (446). If you know of a complete Tetris below 209 bytes, I would like to hear of it.

credit and provenance

The design is pellsson's: 62 of the 104 instruction lines in microtris.asm are his, in the same order (difflib over
the instruction text; comments, labels and directives ignored). tinytris.com is not in this repo, since the upstream
repo has no licence: make fetches it and checks the sha256.

This was written with Claude Code (Anthropic's coding assistant). The design choices, the rules and the numbers are
in the repo; what it claims is checked by `make test`, which does not depend on who wrote what.

licence

LICENSE has the details. The test directory, the Makefile and the documents are copyright 2026 Om Singh, AGPL-3.0 or
later. microtris.asm and the .COM are a derived work of tinytris, which has no licence, so that file is published for
credit and study and is not offered under any licence. If pellsson adds a licence to tinytris, or asks for the parts
taken from it to go, that is what happens here. STYLE says how the repo is kept.
